Warning Signs of Social Engineering
Common Warning Signs
Messages demanding immediate action.
Requests for passwords or personal information.
Unexpected emails containing suspicious links.
Unknown people requesting access to restricted areas.
Messages offering unrealistic prizes or rewards.
Callers pretending to be bank employees or IT support.
Requests to bypass normal security procedures.
Examples
Receiving an email claiming your bank account will be closed unless you click a link.
Receiving a phone call from someone pretending to be technical support and requesting your password.
Finding an unknown USB device and being encouraged to connect it to your computer.
How to Respond
Stay calm and do not rush.
Verify the person's identity.
Do not share confidential information.
Report suspicious messages or behaviour.
Follow your organisation's security procedures.
Conclusion
Recognising warning signs is an important step in preventing social engineering attacks.
